# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S1 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have "borrowed_terms" and "borrowed_terms → controversies"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 has "borrowed_terms → controversies"; they differ when nothing is true, which the second allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S1: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "men are used to speak of the nature and operations of the mind in terms borrowed from sensible ideas" and "men are engaged in controversies and mistakes with regard to the nature and operations of the mind" are false. Is S1, "But, nothing seems more to have contributed towards engaging men in controversies and mistakes with regard to the nature and operations of the mind, than the being used to speak of those things in terms borrowed from sensible ideas.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have nothing from it; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 has "belief_ball → moral_errors"; they differ when only "it is believed that the mind of man is as a ball in motion, impelled and determined by the objects of sense as necessarily as a ball by the stroke of a racket" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2, to: Suppose "it is believed that the mind of man is as a ball in motion, impelled and determined by the objects of sense as necessarily as a ball by the stroke of a racket" is true and "endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence in morality arise" is false. Is S3, "Hence arise endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence in morality.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 has nothing from it; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have "consider_meaning → errors_cleared"; they differ when only "philosophers retire into themselves and attentively consider their own meaning" is true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "philosophers retire into themselves and attentively consider their own meaning" is true and "all these errors are cleared and truth appears plain, uniform and consistent" is false. Is S4, "All which, I doubt not, may be cleared, and truth appear plain, uniform, and consistent, could but philosophers be prevailed on to retire into themselves, and attentively consider their own meaning.", true then?
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have "belief_ball → moral_errors"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 has nothing from it; they differ when only "it is believed that the mind of man is as a ball in motion, impelled and determined by the objects of sense as necessarily as a ball by the stroke of a racket" is true, which the second allows
berkeley-principles-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 has "consider_meaning → errors_cleared"; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 have "moral_errors"; they differ when only "philosophers retire into themselves and attentively consider their own meaning" and "all these errors are cleared and truth appears plain, uniform and consistent" are true, which the first allows
berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2 puts it at S4, "All which, I doubt not, may be cleared, and truth appear plain, uniform, and consistent, could but philosophers be prevailed on to retire into themselves, and attentively consider their own meaning."; berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.2 and berkeley-principles-4.logic.r2.3 at S3, "Hence arise endless scruples and errors of dangerous consequence in morality."
